<p>
  PhonePad turns an Android phone into a wireless controller for a Windows PC.
  Windows does not see a phone pretending to be a keyboard; it sees an Xbox 360
  controller on an XInput slot, the same kind of device a game looks for when it
  asks whether a pad is plugged in. The phone talks to the PC over the Wi-Fi
  network the two already share. There is no Bluetooth pairing, no cable and
  nothing to buy.
</p>
<p>
  Version 1.0.0 was released on 13 September 2026. It is free, its source is
  published under the GNU GPL-3.0, and this article explains what it is for,
  how the parts fit together, and what it chooses not to do.
</p>

<h2>The problem it was built for</h2>
<p>
  A growing share of PC gaming assumes a controller. Console ports often treat
  keyboard and mouse as an afterthought, and Xbox Cloud Gaming streams console
  games that cannot get past their title screen without a gamepad. Most people
  who run into that have no controller in the house, but almost all of them have
  a phone within reach.
</p>
<p>
  The obvious shortcuts do not hold up. A keyboard-mapping app is not a
  controller as far as a game is concerned: the game reads XInput, not key
  presses, and analogue sticks and triggers have no keyboard equivalent. A
  Bluetooth approach needs a Bluetooth radio in the PC, which many desktops do
  not have, including the one PhonePad was built on. What does work is a small
  program on the PC that creates a <em>virtual</em> controller and feeds it
  whatever the phone sends over the network. At the level a game looks, there is
  no difference between that and a pad on a USB cable.
</p>

<Figure caption="The controller in landscape, on the Standard preset. Every control can be moved, resized, rotated, relabelled or hidden in the layout editor." wide panel={false}>
