<h2>Three parts</h2>
<p>PhonePad is three pieces of software with one job between them.</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>The app</strong>, for Android, is the controller itself: sticks,
    triggers, a d-pad and buttons drawn on the screen. Nothing about the layout
    is fixed. A layout editor moves, resizes, rotates and relabels every control,
    and tunes how each one responds. Four presets ship (Standard, Racing,
    Precision and Southpaw), and each layout keeps separate arrangements for
    landscape and portrait, switching when the phone turns.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The companion</strong>, for Windows, is a single self-contained
    program with no installer and no runtime to install first. It creates the
    virtual controller through the ViGEmBus driver, checks and applies what the
    phone sends, and shows what is going on: round trip, packet rate, loss, and
    the reason for anything it rejected.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The protocol</strong> between them is written down in the
    repository and tested from both sides against the same fixture file, so the
    phone and the PC cannot quietly drift apart. It is published under the Apache
    licence rather than the GPL, so anyone can write a compatible client or
    companion without their own work falling under the GPL.
  </li>
</ul>

<Figure caption="Input goes from the phone to the companion over your own network; the companion drives a virtual Xbox 360 controller that Windows treats as hardware. Game rumble travels back to the phone's motor." wide>
