</Figure>

<h2>Setting it up</h2>
<p>Setup is deliberately short.</p>
<ol>
  <li>
    On the PC, install the ViGEmBus driver once. It is what allows a program to
    create a controller that Windows can see.
  </li>
  <li>
    Run the companion. Windows asks whether to let it through the firewall on
    private networks; it needs that to hear the phone. The companion then waits,
    showing a six-digit pairing code.
  </li>
  <li>
    Install the app from the same release, open it, and the PC appears within a
    second or two. Tap it, type the six digits, and the phone becomes the
    controller. After the first pairing, opening the app and tapping the PC is
    the whole procedure.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  The quickest check that it worked is Windows' own game controller panel,
  <code>joy.cpl</code>, which lists an "Xbox 360 Controller for Windows" whose
  buttons and axes light up as the phone is touched.
</p>

