<h2>The cloud gaming detail</h2>
<p>
  Xbox Cloud Gaming works, with one thing worth knowing, because it explains a
  failure that otherwise looks like a bug. A cloud gaming client binds a
  controller to a streaming session at the moment it sees one
  <em>arrive</em>. A pad that already existed when the stream started produces no
  arrival, so the session ends up with no controller bound to it, even though the
  client can read the pad perfectly well. The symptom is specific: the Xbox app's
  own menus respond, and the game ignores everything.
</p>
<p>
  So PhonePad has a way to arrive on demand. Holding the Xbox button on the phone
  for a second unplugs the virtual pad and plugs it back in, which produces a
  genuine arrival without dropping the connection. The companion can also do it
  automatically when it notices a cloud game starting. That option is off by
  default, because noticing works by looking at which programs are running,
  which is a guess, and a wrong guess unplugs a controller someone may be using.
</p>

<h2>How it is meant to feel</h2>
<p>
  The touch sticks were rebuilt rather than tuned, and the reason says a lot
  about the approach. A physical stick needs a deadzone around its centre to hide
  spring slop and sensor drift. A touchscreen has neither, and the game at the
  other end is already applying a deadzone of its own on the assumption that a
  physical stick is attached. PhonePad originally added a second deadzone on top,
  and the result was that roughly the first quarter of the thumb's travel did
  nothing at all before the response arrived in a rush. Now the output starts
  just past the game's own dead band the moment the thumb leaves the centre, and
  the rest of the travel is spent on the range the game responds to. How much
  compensation to apply can be tuned per control. The measured before-and-after
  curves are in <a href="/articles/inside-phonepad/">Inside PhonePad</a>.
</p>
<p>
  The triggers are analogue by default: sliding a thumb along one gives every
  value between released and fully pressed, and a tap is still a full press.
  Game rumble reaches the phone too. A phone has one vibration motor
  where a controller has two, so the strong and weak motors are combined into
  one.
</p>

<h2>What it refuses to do</h2>
<p>Some of PhonePad's most important decisions are about what is not there.</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>No account, no server, no telemetry.</strong> The phone talks to the
    PC on your own network and to nothing else. Pairing establishes a key, and
    every packet is authenticated with it, so nothing else on the network can
    inject input.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No paywall on the controller.</strong> The free app is the product.
    If paid extras ever appear, the project's roadmap commits to them being
    additions rather than a charge for what already works.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No iOS version.</strong> Not for lack of interest: there is no way to
    ship this on iOS that works as well as it does on Android, and doing it badly
    would be worse than not doing it.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No numbers it has not measured.</strong> The latency, packet rate and
    loss figures quoted for PhonePad each come from a recorded run on real
    hardware, and the documentation lists what has not been measured yet, such as
    battery drain and behaviour on a congested network.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  For the record, the measured figures on the development hardware (a Galaxy
  S24 Ultra and a Windows 11 PC on ordinary home Wi-Fi) are a round trip of 4.9 to
  8.0 milliseconds, typically about 5, a steady 249 packets a second, and no loss
  across 42,805 consecutive packets.
</p>

<h2>Who it is for</h2>
<p>
  The plainest answer is someone with a Windows PC, an Android phone and no
  controller, who wants to play something that expects one. That includes Xbox
  Cloud Gaming in a browser, controller-first games on Steam or Game Pass,
  emulators, and browser games that use the Gamepad API.
</p>
<p>
  It is also for people who want to try a controller-heavy game before deciding
  to buy a pad, and for tinkerers: the protocol is documented and openly
  licensed, the layout format is plain data, and the source for both sides is
  public.
</p>

<h2>What it does not do yet</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Gyro aiming is not implemented. The protocol reserves a flag for it; nothing else exists yet.</li>
  <li>One phone at a time, so no local two-player.</li>
  <li>
    The Windows companion is not code-signed, so SmartScreen warns on first run.
    Checksums for every release file are published alongside it.
  </li>
  <li>
    ViGEmBus, the driver underneath, was archived by its author in 2023. It works
    well and is what Parsec and DS4Windows rely on, and PhonePad keeps it behind
    a single interface so that a successor can replace it without touching
    anything else.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  The roadmap has no dates. In roughly the order they are likely to happen:
  per-game tuning profiles with a short calibration step, a portable format for
  sharing layouts, starting the companion at login, gyro aiming, and a second
  phone.
</p>

<h2>Who is using it</h2>
<p>
  PhonePad 1.0 came out on 13 September 2026 with no launch and no promotion,
  and people have been finding it on their own. By 8 November the Android app
  had been downloaded from GitHub 939 times and the Windows companion 553 times.
  In the thirty days to 7 November its website had about nine times as many
  visitors as in the thirty days before, arriving through search engines and,
  in the most recent week more than anywhere else, through links from ChatGPT.
</p>
<p>
  People using it have started asking for what it does not do yet. The first
  outside report, on 3 November, was that a phone sharing its own mobile hotspot
  with the PC never finds the companion; a second user confirmed it the same
  day, and the person who reported it followed up with a pull request proposing
  a fix. That is the kind of feedback the roadmap above is meant to absorb.
</p>

<h2>Getting it</h2>
<p>
  PhonePad is not on the Play Store. Releases are published on
  <a href="https://github.com/MNBLabs/phonepad/releases" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>:
  an Android APK for 64-bit ARM phones and the Windows companion, with a SHA-256
  checksum file. The <a href="https://phonepad.dynshift.com/setup/">setup guide</a>
  walks through the first connection, and
  <a href="https://phonepad.dynshift.com/troubleshooting/">troubleshooting</a>
  covers firewalls, discovery and the rest. Bugs go to the repository's issues,
  and questions and ideas to its discussions.
</p>

